000. | Ratio Chasing to prove AI:AJ = AJ:AI:
| Premise AI = AJ gives a linear equation with coefficients {AI²: 1, AJ²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AI²: 2, AJ²: -2} that gives AI:AJ = AJ:AI



006. | Angle Chasing to prove ∠(AD,AF) = ∠(AJ,AE):
| Premise ∠(AB,AI) = ∠(AI,AC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: -1, AC: -1, AI: 2}
| Premise ∠(AI,IJ) = ∠(IJ,AJ) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AI: -1, AJ: -1, IJ: 2}
| Then {AB: 1, AC: 1, AI: -2} + -1 x {AI: -1, AJ: -1, IJ: 2}
|    = {AB: 1, AC: 1, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2}
| Premise AB ∥ AF gives a linear equation with coefficients {AB: 1, AF: -1}
| Then {AB: 1, AC: 1, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2} + -1 x {AB: 1, AF: -1}
|    = {AC: 1, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1}
| Premise AC ∥ AD gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, AD: -1}
| Then {AC: 1, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1} + 1 x {AC: 1, AD: -1}
|    = {AC: 2, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1, AD: -1}
| Premise AI ∥ AE gives a linear equation with coefficients {AE: 1, AI: -1}
| Then {AC: 2, AI: -1, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1, AD: -1} + -1 x {AE: 1, AI: -1}
|    = {AC: 2, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1, AD: -1, AE: -1}
| Premise AC ⟂ IJ gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
| Then {AC: 2, AJ: 1, IJ: -2, AF: 1, AD: -1, AE: -1} + -2 x {AC: 1, IJ: -1}
|    = {AJ: 1, AF: 1, AD: -1, AE: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AJ: 1, AF: 1, AD: -1, AE: -1} that gives ∠(AD,AF) = ∠(AJ,AE)



